course: dictionary
lesson: 6197
title: Misleading words and claims
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Something is misleading when it gives people a wrong idea, even if every word in it is true. «Headlines are written to make you click and can be misleading, so read beyond the headline.» The headline may be true in a small way, but the wrong idea comes from what it leaves out.
explain: Misleading can also be a verb. To mislead someone is to lead them the wrong way or make them believe something false. «A half-truth is partly true but misleading.» A half-truth can mislead you without being a complete lie.
explain: Look for clues in the context. A misleading graph, such as a bar chart whose scale does not start at zero, can make small differences look huge. Ask yourself: what idea is this really giving me, and is anything missing?
fact: Misleading means giving people a wrong idea.
fact: Something can be misleading even if not every word in it is a lie.
fact: «Misleading» is an adjective, and «mislead» is the verb: to lead someone the wrong way or make them believe something false.
fact: «The famous picture of a line of apes turning into a man is misleading», because evolution is a branching bush, not a ladder.
fact: Fact-checkers check claims, look for evidence, ask experts and rate claims as true, false or misleading.
exercise: «Headlines are written to make you click and can be misleading.» What does misleading mean here? | Printed in bold letters | Very long and hard to read | Making people laugh | *Giving people a wrong idea
exercise: «A half-truth is partly true but misleading.» Can something be misleading without being a lie? | *Yes, a half-truth can give a wrong idea even though it is partly true | No, only lies can ever be misleading | Yes, but only if it is written in capital letters | No, because true words always give a clear idea
exercise: «To mislead is to make someone believe something wrong.» What does mislead mean? | To write a long story | To agree with everyone in the room | To find the right way home | *To lead someone the wrong way or make them believe something false
test: A bar chart has a scale that does not start at zero. Why might it be misleading? | It shows only the year | It is always coloured in red | It has too many numbers to read | *It can make small differences look huge
test: Which sentence shows a misleading advert? | An advert with a picture of a red apple only | An advert that tells you the exact price and the opening times | *An advert that uses a true fact but hides the important detail to sell a product | An advert that says the shop is closed on Sunday
exam: «The famous picture of a line of apes turning into a man is misleading.» Why? | Because the picture is in black and white | Because it shows apes are not animals | Because apes cannot walk on two legs | *Because evolution is a branching bush, not a ladder, so the picture gives a wrong idea
